  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard, the rule, and the panel rename were each verified by reading them back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view 'Kibana Sample Data Logs'** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) has 45 fields. The key ones are `timestamp` (date), `response` (string, with `response.keyword` for exact matching), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, `host`, and `agent`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard 'S4-73ffc5d1 Web logs'** (id `dcdd2b37-2131-4d61-82e5-7457fb8818dd`) has exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count over a `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a pie chart of count grouped by `response.keyword`.\n   \n   I chose a 7-day default time range and a pie chart for the response-code panel. The API rejected a bar chart split by terms, so the pie was the closest valid option.\n\n3. **Rule 'S4-73ffc5d1 5xx errors'** (id `66f4b9eb-7792-49b7-a09e-1df021196b02`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the web-logs data view. It runs every 1m, counts matches over a 5m window, and fires when the count is greater than 10. The query is KQL: `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`. This is a string range, which is exact here because every response code is three digits. It was created enabled, so no enable call was needed. Its last execution finished with status `ok`.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** the response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". The other panel is unchanged. I read the rule back: `enabled: true`.\n\nI could not run a direct Elasticsearch query to list the distinct `response` values, because the ES|QL endpoint and the console proxy are unavailable on this Kibana. The 5xx filter is therefore based on the three-digit format, not on a value count.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
